WB.L("cane-toads", "nature", "beginner", "Cane toads: the pest that conquered the north",
  "In 1935 a hundred-odd toads were shipped to Queensland to save the sugar crop. They ignored the beetles they were meant to eat and set off across a continent.",
  [
    ["A plan made in hope", "Cane toads were brought to Queensland in 1935 to eat cane beetles, but they could not reach them.",
      "In the early 1930s, grubs of native cane beetles were chewing the roots of Queensland's sugar crop. The cane toad, a hardy amphibian from Central and South America, had a reputation for eating plantation pests elsewhere. In June 1935, 102 toads were brought from Hawaii to Gordonvale, near Cairns. They bred fast, and thousands of young toads were soon released around the cane fields. Some scientists warned that releasing an untested predator was reckless. They were ignored.\n\nThe plan had a basic flaw. Adult beetles sit high on the cane stalks and their grubs live underground, but toads are heavy ground hunters that cannot climb. There is no good evidence they dented beetle numbers. They ate almost everything else instead."],
    ["Built to invade", "Huge clutches of eggs, a tough body and few natural enemies let cane toads spread across northern Australia.",
      "Cane toads have almost every trait an invader needs. A female can lay tens of thousands of eggs in one clutch, in long strings of jelly in still water. Tadpoles develop quickly, and adults tolerate heat, drought and habitats from wetlands to suburban backyards. In their native range, parasites, diseases and predators that evolved alongside them keep numbers in check. In Australia almost none of those controls exist. The countryside around the cane farms offered insects, frogs, small mammals and pet food. Toads spread south into New South Wales and west across the Northern Territory, reaching the Kimberley by the late 2000s. They now cover well over a million square kilometres, and the western front is still moving."],
    ["Evolution in fast forward", "Toads at the invasion front have evolved to travel faster, which speeds up the spread.",
      "The invasion is accelerating. In the early decades the front advanced about ten kilometres a year. By the 2000s, toads in the Northern Territory were advancing at more than five times that rate. Biologist Rick Shine and colleagues found why. The toads leading the charge had longer legs than those in older populations, and they travelled further each night in straighter lines. The reason is simple selection. The fastest toads reach new ground first, where food is plentiful and competitors are few, and they breed with other fast toads. Generation after generation, the front became a population of athletes. It is one of the clearest examples of evolution within living memory."],
    ["Poison on the menu", "Cane toad toxin kills many native predators that never evolved to cope with it.",
      "Large parotoid glands behind a toad's head hold a poison of chemicals called bufadienolides, which attack the heart. Every life stage is toxic, including eggs and tadpoles. Predators in the toads' native range have had millions of years to adapt, but Australian predators had never met one. Goannas, freshwater crocodiles, northern quolls, some snakes and pet dogs die after biting or swallowing a toad. When the front reaches a new area, some goanna populations can crash dramatically. Not every native animal loses, though. Crows and kites have learned to flip toads over and eat the safer innards, and native water rats have been seen doing something similar."],
    ["Fighting back cleverly", "Since toads cannot be removed, scientists now work to reduce harm and teach predators to avoid them.",
      "Nobody has found a way to remove cane toads from Australia, so the focus is on limiting the damage. One clever idea uses the toads' own biology. Cane toad tadpoles are strongly attracted to a chemical from toad toxin, because they hunt and eat newly laid toad eggs. Traps baited with that chemical catch huge numbers of tadpoles while leaving native tadpoles largely alone. Another approach is taste aversion training. Northern quolls and goannas have been fed small toads or toad sausages that make them sick but do not kill them, so they learn to avoid toads before the invasion arrives. If you catch toads at home, wildlife authorities recommend humane methods, such as cooling them in a fridge and then freezing them."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the cane toads fail to control the cane beetles?", ["The beetles were poisonous to the toads","The beetles lived where toads could not reach","Toads hibernated through the beetle season","Farmers killed the toads before they bred"], 1, "Heavy, ground-dwelling toads could not climb the stalks or dig to the grubs, so they ate other things."],
    [1, "Why did cane toads spread so quickly across Australia?", ["A cooler, wetter climate than their homeland suited them","Huge egg clutches, hardiness and few natural enemies","Farmers kept moving them north by truck","They fed only on native cane pests"], 1, "A tough body, huge clutches of eggs and the lack of parasites and predators let numbers surge."],
    [2, "Why are toads at the invasion front faster than toads in older populations?", ["They eat a much richer diet on fresh ground","Warmer weather in the north speeds them up","The fastest toads arrive first and breed together","They belong to a separate, faster species"], 2, "Selection favours speed at the front, so each generation there runs faster than the last."],
    [3, "Why do native predators such as goannas die after eating cane toads?", ["Toads carry a parasite that spreads to them","Toad spines badly injure their throats","Toad meat is far too tough to digest","They have no evolved resistance to the poison"], 3, "Australian predators had never met a toad, so they have no defence against its toxin."],
    [4, "How do tadpole traps help control cane toads?", ["A lure of toad toxin attracts toad tadpoles","Bright lights draw tadpoles in at night","They poison all the water around them","They play recordings of male toad calls"], 0, "Toad tadpoles are drawn to the toxin chemical because they eat toad eggs, so traps largely spare native tadpoles."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"They were shipped in to guard a crop and ignored the pest. What did they do instead?"});

WB.L("bioluminescence", "nature", "beginner", "Living light: the glow of bioluminescence",
  "In the darkest places on Earth, animals make their own light. They use it to hunt, hide, flirt and even call the police on their attackers.",
  [
    ["Light from chemistry", "Bioluminescence is cold light made by a chemical reaction in a living thing, and most of it happens in the sea.",
      "Bioluminescence is cold light made by life. Usually a molecule called luciferin reacts with oxygen, helped by an enzyme called luciferase, and the energy comes out as light rather than heat. Different groups evolved their own versions, so glowing has arisen independently dozens of times. Some animals make the light themselves. Many deep-sea fish instead house glowing bacteria in special organs.\n\nThe sea is where it thrives. Off California, researchers found roughly three-quarters of the deep-water animals they observed could make light. Most is blue or blue-green, which travels furthest in sea water. Reds fade within metres. Some dragonfish make red light near their eyes and can see it, a private searchlight for prey that cannot."],
    ["Hiding in plain light", "Some animals glow on their undersides to erase their own shadow, a trick called counter-illumination.",
      "In the ocean's twilight zone, faint light filters down from above. Any animal swimming there casts a dark silhouette that predators below can spot. Many fish, squid and shrimps solve this by lighting up their undersides to match the glow overhead, so their outline disappears. This is called counter-illumination. Hatchetfish use rows of light organs along their bellies. The Hawaiian bobtail squid uses glowing bacteria called Vibrio fischeri, held in a light organ. Each morning it expels most of them and regrows the colony, and it can adjust its brightness to match moonlight. It is invisibility achieved by adding light rather than taking it away."],
    ["Lures, alarms, smoke screens", "Light can be used as bait, as a burglar alarm, or as a smoke screen to confuse a predator.",
      "The deep-sea anglerfish dangles a bacteria-lit lure from a modified fin spine on its head and swallows whatever comes to investigate. Light also serves defence. Tiny plankton flash when disturbed, and biologists have proposed a burglar alarm effect: the flash may attract larger predators that attack whatever is attacking the plankton. Certain jellyfish give off pinwheels of light when grabbed, perhaps for the same reason. Others use light as a smoke screen. Some deep-sea shrimps and squid squirt glowing fluid instead of dark ink, and the vampire squid can release sticky, glowing mucus. A predator lunging at the bright cloud may miss the animal slipping away into the dark."],
    ["Fireflies and glow-worms", "On land, glowing insects use light to find mates or to trap food.",
      "Fireflies are actually beetles, and their flashes are a courtship language. Males flash species-specific patterns in flight, and females answer from the ground with timed signals. Some females exploit this. In North America, females of the genus Photuris mimic the replies of another genus, lure in eager males and eat them.\n\nAustralia and New Zealand have a different light show. The glow-worms of caves and damp gullies, such as Springbrook in Queensland or Waitomo in New Zealand, are not worms but the larvae of fungus gnats. They hang sticky silk threads from the ceiling and glow blue to attract small flying insects, which become trapped and are reeled in."],
    ["Glowing seas, borrowed light", "Glowing plankton light up beaches, and borrowed glowing proteins have transformed biology.",
      "Sometimes the glow comes to the shore. Tiny single-celled plankton called dinoflagellates flash when disturbed, so breaking waves, paddling feet and kayak strokes can light up electric blue. Beaches in Tasmania, Jervis Bay and many places overseas occasionally put on this display. Some fungi glow too, including Australia's ghost fungus, which shines a faint green on rotting wood at night.\n\nScientists have borrowed nature's lights. Firefly luciferase is used in laboratories to detect chemical activity, and a green fluorescent protein from a jellyfish became a revolutionary tool for tagging cells and genes, earning the 2008 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. A glow first noticed in the sea now helps researchers watch diseases develop."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What usually produces the light in bioluminescence?", ["Waste heat released from the animal's body","Electricity stored in special skin cells","Luciferin reacting with oxygen and an enzyme","Moonlight reflected off special scales"], 2, "The energy of the reaction is released as light rather than heat, which makes the glow remarkably efficient."],
    [1, "What is counter-illumination?", ["Flashing to warn others of danger","Glowing underneath to hide your silhouette","Using light to attract mates","Squirting clouds of glowing fluid"], 1, "By matching the faint light from above, an animal's shadow disappears to predators looking up."],
    [2, "Why might a flash from disturbed plankton help protect them?", ["It blinds the attacker for good","It warms the water so predators leave","It may call bigger predators to the attacker","It tells nearby plankton to breed"], 2, "This burglar alarm idea suggests the flash calls in bigger predators that turn on the attacker."],
    [3, "What are the glow-worms of Australian and New Zealand caves, and why do they glow?", ["Firefly larvae that glow to find mates","Earthworms that glow to warn predators","Fungus gnat larvae that glow to lure prey","Beetle larvae that glow to scare off birds"], 2, "They are fungus gnat larvae that glow blue to draw small flying insects into silk threads."],
    [4, "What causes the glowing waves seen on some beaches?", ["Tiny plankton that flash when disturbed","Radioactive minerals in the wet sand","Phosphorus washing off coastal rocks","Jellyfish stinging in the shallows"], 0, "Dinoflagellates flash when the water moves, so waves and footsteps light up blue."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In the dark deep sea, glowing is common. Why would an animal glow to turn invisible?"});

WB.L("elephants", "nature", "beginner", "Elephants: memory, matriarchs and the meaning of death",
  "Elephants live in families led by wise old females, talk in rumbles too low for us to hear, and pay strange attention to their dead.",
  [
    ["Three species, big lives", "There are three living elephant species: African savanna, African forest and Asian elephants.",
      "Genetic studies show Africa holds two elephant species, not one. The savanna elephant is the largest land animal alive, with big males up to about four metres at the shoulder and six tonnes or more. The forest elephant is smaller, with straighter, downward-pointing tusks, and lives in central and west African rainforest. The IUCN began assessing them separately in 2021. The Asian elephant, with smaller ears and a domed head, is a different branch again, more closely related to the extinct woolly mammoth than to either African species. Elephants eat well over a hundred kilograms a day and can live into their sixties. They dig waterholes, open thick bush and, in forests, spread seeds in their dung."],
    ["Led by grandmothers", "Elephant families are led by an older female, and her experience can mean life or death.",
      "Elephant society is built around females. A family of related mothers, daughters, sisters and young is led by an older matriarch. Young males leave in their early to mid teens. Pregnancy lasts around 22 months, the longest of any land mammal, and the calf, about a hundred kilograms at birth, is helped by aunts and older sisters. Research in Kenya's Amboseli National Park showed why the matriarch matters. Families led by older females were better at telling familiar calls from strangers' and reacted most strongly to the most dangerous lion roars. Older matriarchs also remember routes to water in drought. When poaching kills them, a lifetime of knowledge is lost."],
    ["Talking through the ground", "Elephants communicate with deep rumbles, some below the range of human hearing, and may sense vibrations through the ground.",
      "Elephants trumpet, but much of their conversation is quieter to our ears. They use deep rumbles, part of which are infrasound, frequencies too low for humans to hear. These sounds travel far, helping scattered groups keep in touch and males find females ready to mate. Researchers have also found evidence that elephants detect vibrations travelling through the ground. Their feet and trunks are packed with sensitive nerve endings, and elephants sometimes freeze, lean forward and press a foot down as if listening. The trunk is a marvel too: a boneless organ of muscle that can pluck a single blade of grass, suck up litres of water or lift a heavy log, with an extraordinary sense of smell."],
    ["Minds and mourning", "Elephants seem to recognise themselves in mirrors and show unusual interest in their dead, though elephant graveyards are a myth.",
      "Elephants are among the few animals that seem to recognise themselves in a mirror. In a study published in 2006, an Asian elephant named Happy at the Bronx Zoo repeatedly touched a painted mark on her head that she could see only in her reflection. Elephants also cooperate on tasks, swat flies with branches and remember individuals for years.\n\nThey treat their dead unusually too. They touch bodies, stand quietly beside them and return over several days. In Amboseli, elephants showed far more interest in elephant skulls and ivory than in other skulls or wood. Whether this is grief is still debated. The old story of elephant graveyards is a myth, though bones can gather near water where weak animals linger."],
    ["Evolution by poaching", "Heavy poaching in Gorongosa left far more tuskless females, a clear case of evolution driven by hunting.",
      "Humans have shaped elephants too. Ivory poaching targets animals with tusks. In Mozambique's Gorongosa National Park, heavy poaching during the civil war from 1977 to 1992 left a population with a far higher share of tuskless females, because tuskless animals were more likely to survive and breed. Genetic research published in 2021 confirmed this was evolution driven by hunting. It is a sobering reminder of how quickly human pressure can change a species, and of the knowledge lost whenever older elephants are killed."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why are elephants now generally counted as three species rather than two?", ["Asian elephants were found to be two species","Genetic studies show two distinct African species","Forest elephants are just young savanna elephants","Savanna elephants vary a lot in size by country"], 1, "African savanna, African forest and Asian elephants are now treated as three distinct species."],
    [1, "What did research in Amboseli show about older matriarchs?", ["They are more aggressive towards all visitors","They leave the family once they grow old","Their families respond better to real threats","They stop communicating with the herd"], 2, "Families led by older matriarchs were better at judging which calls and lion roars were genuinely dangerous."],
    [2, "How do elephants keep in touch over long distances?", ["High-pitched trumpets carrying for kilometres","Chemical signals left in their dung trails","Tapping their tusks against large trees","Deep rumbles, partly too low for us to hear"], 3, "Low sounds travel well over long distances, so rumbles help scattered groups stay in contact."],
    [3, "What is true of the so-called elephant graveyards?", ["They are documented sites elephants walk to before dying","They are a myth, though bones may gather near water","They are protected by law in Kenya","They are used only by Asian elephants"], 1, "There are no special graveyards; bones may build up near water where weak animals linger."],
    [4, "Why does Gorongosa National Park have many tuskless female elephants?", ["A disease weakened and destroyed their tusks","Poaching favoured the tuskless survivors","They were bred that way in captivity","Tusks wear away in hot, dry climates"], 1, "Poaching killed tusked elephants, so tuskless ones survived to breed, and genetic work confirmed the shift."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Elephants talk in sounds we cannot hear, and they notice the dead. What else do they do?"});

WB.L("emperor-penguins", "nature", "beginner", "Emperor penguins: fathers of the Antarctic winter",
  "While most birds leave the Antarctic winter behind, emperor penguins walk into it to breed. The fathers then stand in the dark for two months with an egg on their feet.",
  [
    ["The biggest penguin", "Emperor penguins are the largest penguins and the only penguins that breed through the Antarctic winter.",
      "Emperor penguins stand a little over a metre tall and can weigh more than 30 kilograms when well fed, making them the largest living penguins. They live only around Antarctica and spend most of their lives at sea, hunting fish, krill and squid. What makes them unique is their timing. Most Antarctic animals breed in the brief summer, when food is plentiful and temperatures are mild. Emperors do the opposite. In autumn they march, waddle and toboggan on their bellies across the sea ice to traditional breeding sites, sometimes many kilometres from open water. There, in the coldest, darkest season on the planet, they court, pair up and raise a single chick."],
    ["Two months in the dark", "The female passes her single egg to the male, who incubates it for about two months without eating.",
      "After courtship, around May or June, the female lays one large egg. Weakened by fasting, she must return to the sea to feed, so the egg is passed to her partner. This is delicate: if it rolls onto the ice, it can freeze and the embryo dies. The male balances it on his feet under a warm brood pouch, and she sets off on her long trek.\n\nFor about 65 days he stands through the polar night, in temperatures that can fall below minus 40 degrees Celsius, eating nothing. Counting courtship, he may fast around four months, living on stored fat and moving as little as possible. If his partner is late, he can produce a nourishing throat secretion for the newly hatched chick."],
    ["The power of the huddle", "Thousands of penguins huddle and slowly shuffle so that everyone takes a turn in the warmth.",
      "Alone, a male emperor would struggle to survive the winter. Together they manage it. In the worst weather, males press tightly into huddles of thousands of birds. Inside a dense huddle the temperature can climb above 30 degrees Celsius, so warm that birds sometimes break away to cool down. Huddles are not static. Researchers filming colonies found the birds shuffle in small, coordinated steps, sending slow waves through the group, so the huddle gradually moves and reorganises. Birds on the windy edge work inwards while others drift outwards. The result is a remarkably fair system, in which no bird stays in the warm centre or on the freezing edge for too long."],
    ["The worst journey", "Explorers once risked their lives in the polar winter to collect emperor eggs.",
      "The emperors' winter breeding was so mysterious that scientists once took terrible risks to study it. In 1911, on Robert Falcon Scott's Antarctic expedition, Edward Wilson, Henry Bowers and Apsley Cherry-Garrard set out in the winter darkness for the emperor colony at Cape Crozier. They hoped the embryos would reveal clues to how birds evolved from reptiles. In temperatures below minus 50 degrees Celsius, they man-hauled sledges for weeks, lost their tent in a blizzard and nearly died. They returned with three eggs. Wilson and Bowers later perished with Scott on the return from the South Pole. Cherry-Garrard told the story in his classic book The Worst Journey in the World."],
    ["Seen from space", "Satellites now count emperor colonies, and shrinking sea ice is their greatest threat.",
      "Colonies are remote, so scientists now find many from space. The birds leave large brown stains of guano on the white ice, visible in satellite images, and high-resolution pictures can help estimate numbers. Surveys have revealed colonies no one knew existed. The same technology shows the danger. Emperors need stable sea ice from autumn until their chicks grow waterproof feathers in summer. In 2022, early sea ice break-up in one region of west Antarctica likely killed almost all chicks in several colonies. Because sea ice is shrinking as the climate warms, many scientists warn that emperor numbers could fall steeply this century."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes the breeding of emperor penguins unusual?", ["They breed on sea ice in winter","They breed only on rocky islands","They lay their eggs in the water","They breed only once every five years"], 0, "Emperors are unique in breeding through the Antarctic winter on sea ice, not in summer."],
    [1, "How does the male survive about two months of incubation?", ["He eats snow and ice for energy","He takes shifts at sea with other males","He lives off stored fat and barely moves","He feeds on fish kept in his brood pouch"], 2, "He eats nothing for around 65 days, living on stored fat while keeping the egg off the ice."],
    [2, "How do emperor huddles stay fair to all the birds?", ["The strongest birds stay in the middle","Birds shuffle so positions slowly rotate","Females push males out of the centre","Huddles only form for a few minutes"], 1, "Small coordinated movements send waves through the huddle, moving birds between the edge and the warm centre."],
    [3, "What did the 1911 winter journey hope to learn from emperor embryos?", ["How penguins survive in water","How colonies find their way home","How birds evolved from reptiles","How the cold affects egg size"], 2, "Scientists believed the embryos might reveal how birds evolved from reptiles, which made them worth a deadly trek."],
    [4, "Why does shrinking sea ice threaten emperor penguins?", ["Chicks need stable ice until fully feathered","Eggs hatch too early in warmer water","Colonies become too crowded to breed","Adults cannot find mates without it"], 0, "Early break-up in 2022 likely killed almost all chicks in several colonies."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Most Antarctic birds leave for winter. Why do emperors breed through it?"});

WB.L("hibernation", "nature", "beginner", "Hibernation: the art of switching off",
  "Some animals survive hard times by turning their bodies almost all the way down. A few cool themselves to below freezing and still wake up.",
  [
    ["More than a long sleep", "Hibernation is a controlled, reversible shutdown of metabolism, and many small animals use shorter daily torpor.",
      "We say animals sleep through winter, but hibernation is different. A hibernator deliberately lowers its metabolism, the rate its body burns energy, to a tiny fraction of normal. Body temperature drops, breathing slows and a ground squirrel's heart may fall from hundreds of beats a minute to a handful. Biologists call this state torpor. Hibernation is long torpor, lasting weeks or months, and the body controls it and can reverse it.\n\nMany small animals use shorter daily torpor. Hummingbirds burn energy so fast that a cold night without food could kill them, so some let their body temperature fall overnight and warm up at dawn. Small Australian marsupials such as dunnarts, some possums and gliders, and many microbats do the same."],
    ["Below freezing and back", "Arctic ground squirrels cool their bodies to below zero without freezing solid.",
      "The Arctic ground squirrel of Alaska and northern Canada holds a remarkable record. During hibernation its body temperature can fall to almost minus three degrees Celsius, the lowest core temperature measured in a mammal. Its body fluids do not freeze, because they are supercooled, staying liquid below the usual freezing point. Even so, the squirrel cannot stay that cold all winter. Like most small hibernators, it wakes every week or few, rewarming to normal temperature for a short time before dropping again. These arousals burn a large share of its stored energy. Scientists still debate why they are needed, with ideas including resetting the immune system or restoring the brain."],
    ["Bears break the rules", "Bears stay fairly warm in winter but go months without eating, drinking or passing waste.",
      "Bears were once said not to be true hibernators, because their body temperature drops only a few degrees. Today most biologists count them as hibernators of a special kind. A black or brown bear in its den slows its metabolism dramatically while staying relatively warm, which helps a mother give birth and nurse tiny cubs in mid-winter. The astonishing part is what bears do without. They can go for months without eating, drinking, urinating or defecating. Their bodies recycle waste products into new protein, and they lose surprisingly little bone or muscle despite lying still. Medical researchers study bears closely, hoping to help bedridden patients or people with kidney disease."],
    ["Australian hibernators", "Echidnas and the mountain pygmy possum are among Australia's true hibernators.",
      "Australia might not seem like hibernation country, but some of its animals do it. Short-beaked echidnas in cooler regions, such as Tasmania and the alpine high country, can hibernate for several months, letting their body temperature fall close to their surroundings. The mountain pygmy possum, a small, endangered marsupial found only in the alpine areas of Victoria and New South Wales, hibernates for months beneath the winter snowpack among boulder fields. In spring and summer it feasts on Bogong moths, which migrate to the mountains, and builds fat for the long sleep. Fewer moths in recent years has worried researchers, because the possums depend on that rich food supply to survive winter."],
    ["Surprises and future uses", "Hibernation turns up in unexpected animals, and learning how it works may one day help medicine.",
      "Hibernation has surprised scientists more than once. In 1946 naturalist Edmund Jaeger found a common poorwill, a North American nightbird, torpid in a rock crevice in winter, showing that at least one bird could hibernate. In 2004 researchers reported that the fat-tailed dwarf lemur of Madagascar hibernates for months in tree holes, the first primate known to do so. These discoveries have sparked medical interest. If doctors could safely slow a human's metabolism, it might protect organs during surgery, buy time for trauma or stroke patients, or one day help astronauts on very long journeys. For now that remains research, not reality. Hibernators remind us that bodies can be far more flexible than we assume."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What best describes hibernation?", ["A very deep, ordinary kind of sleep","A faint caused by extreme cold","Simply staying in a burrow all winter","A controlled, reversible drop in metabolism"], 3, "Hibernation is long-lasting torpor, an actively managed shutdown rather than just deep sleep."],
    [1, "How can an Arctic ground squirrel's body fall below zero without freezing?", ["Its body fluids supercool and stay liquid","Its blood is swapped for plain antifreeze","Its thick fur stops the cold reaching it","Its heart beats faster to keep it warm"], 0, "Supercooled fluids stay liquid below the usual freezing point, giving the lowest core temperature recorded in a mammal."],
    [2, "What can hibernating bears go without for months?", ["Any heartbeat or breathing at all","Staying even slightly warm","Eating, drinking and passing waste","Any change in their metabolism"], 2, "Bears go months without food, water or passing waste, recycling waste products into protein."],
    [3, "Why does the mountain pygmy possum depend on Bogong moths?", ["The moths help it build its winter nest","They give it the rich food that builds fat","They keep predators away from its burrow","The moths signal the start of each spring"], 1, "The possum feasts on migrating Bogong moths in spring and summer, so fewer moths threaten its winter survival."],
    [4, "Why are doctors interested in hibernation?", ["It could replace most surgical procedures","Hibernators never suffer from any disease","Humans will soon be able to hibernate","It might protect organs in surgery or trauma"], 3, "The idea is still research, but slowed metabolism could buy time and protect organs."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"One mammal can cool to below freezing and wake up. How does it avoid turning to ice?"});

WB.L("yellowstone-wolves", "nature", "intermediate", "The wolves of Yellowstone",
  "When wolves returned to Yellowstone in 1995, they became the centre of one of ecology's most famous stories. The truth is messier, and more interesting.",
  [
    ["Keystone species", "Some species have effects on their ecosystem far larger than their numbers suggest.",
      "In the 1960s American ecologist Robert Paine ran a simple experiment on the rocky shore of Washington State. He repeatedly removed a predatory sea star from one stretch of rocks and left a neighbouring stretch alone. Without the sea star, mussels took over, crowding out other species, and the shore's diversity collapsed. Paine called such a species a keystone, after the stone at the top of an arch that holds the rest in place. The idea that predators can shape whole ecosystems through a chain of effects called a trophic cascade became hugely influential. Sea otters, for instance, protect kelp forests by eating sea urchins. Wolves in Yellowstone became the most famous test case of all."],
    ["Lost and returned", "Wolves were deliberately wiped out of Yellowstone by the 1920s, then returned from Canada in 1995 and 1996.",
      "Yellowstone, established in 1872, was the world's first national park, but early managers saw wolves as vermin that killed livestock and the elk and deer visitors liked to see. Government hunters shot, trapped and poisoned them, and by the mid-1920s they were gone. Elk numbers rose, and managers worried about heavy browsing of young aspen, willow and cottonwood. Rangers at times culled elk themselves. Attitudes changed, and after the wolf was listed as endangered, plans to restore it gained momentum despite fierce opposition from ranchers. In 1995 and 1996, 31 wolves from Canada were released into the park, and more went to central Idaho. They thrived, settling at roughly one hundred inside the park, and became a major attraction in the Lamar Valley."],
    ["The famous story", "A popular account says wolves restored trees, beavers and even the shape of rivers.",
      "As the wolves settled in, elk numbers on the northern range fell sharply, from around 19,000 in the mid-1990s to a fraction of that within two decades. Some researchers reported willows and aspens growing taller in places, and beaver colonies increased. A powerful story took shape. Wolves, it was said, not only ate elk but frightened them away from exposed valleys and riverbanks, creating a landscape of fear. Freed from browsing, trees recovered, beavers returned, songbirds followed and riverbanks stabilised. A short online video in 2014, How Wolves Change Rivers, spread this story to many millions of viewers. It is a beautiful narrative of nature healing itself, and parts of it are supported by real evidence."],
    ["What scientists argue about", "The strength of Yellowstone's trophic cascade is still debated by researchers.",
      "Many ecologists think the popular story is too tidy. Elk declined for several reasons at once, including predation by cougars and bears as well as wolves, hunting outside the park and drought. Some studies found elk did not avoid risky places as strongly as the landscape of fear idea suggested, and that aspen often failed to recover even where wolves were common. Other work found willow recovery was patchy and modest. One reason is that when beavers vanished, their dams collapsed, streams cut deeper channels and the water table dropped. Willows on those drier banks struggle however few elk there are. Researchers still disagree on how much of Yellowstone's change can truly be credited to wolves."],
    ["Lessons from the debate", "Wolves clearly changed Yellowstone, but ecosystems rarely follow a simple chain of cause and effect.",
      "Decades of radio-collar data make Yellowstone's wolves among the most studied large predators on Earth. Some effects are well established. Their kills feed ravens, eagles, magpies, bears and coyotes through winter, and wolves have reshaped the behaviour and numbers of other predators. Elk herds are smaller and behave differently. What remains contested is how far those changes flowed down to trees, rivers and the wider landscape. The debate is not a failure of science but science working, as researchers test an appealing idea against messy data. The broader lesson is valuable. Removing a top predator can change an ecosystem for a century, and putting it back does not simply rewind the clock."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a keystone species?", ["One with outsized effects for its numbers","The most numerous species in an ecosystem","A species that lives only on rocky shores","The largest animal in its ecosystem"], 0, "Removing Paine's sea star collapsed the shore's diversity, showing how one predator can hold a community together."],
    [1, "Why did managers worry after wolves were removed from Yellowstone?", ["Visitor numbers fell sharply after the wolves went","Elk numbers rose and browsing harmed young trees","Livestock on nearby ranches began to die","Coyotes disappeared from the whole park"], 1, "Without their main predator, elk increased and browsed young trees heavily."],
    [2, "What is the 'landscape of fear' idea?", ["Visitors keep away from areas with wolves","Wolves keep away from people and roads","Elk avoid risky places where wolves hunt","Rivers flood when wolves are present"], 2, "Fear of wolves is said to change where elk feed, easing browsing pressure on exposed valleys and riverbanks."],
    [3, "Why do many ecologists think the popular wolf story is too tidy?", ["Wolves were never actually in Yellowstone","The video was filmed in a different park","Elk numbers did not change after wolves returned","Several factors cut elk, and recovery was patchy"], 3, "Cougars, bears, hunting and drought all affected elk, and the loss of beaver dams limited willow recovery."],
    [4, "What does the Yellowstone debate teach about removing top predators?", ["Putting a predator back does not simply rewind the clock","Returning a predator always restores an ecosystem quickly","Predators have little effect on ecosystems","Scientific disagreement shows the research has failed"], 0, "Ecosystems rarely follow a simple chain of cause and effect, so some damage repairs along its own path."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The story of wolves reshaping rivers is famous. Scientists say it is more complicated."});

WB.L("spider-silk", "nature", "intermediate", "Spider silk: nature's super-material",
  "Weight for weight, some spider silk is stronger than steel and tougher than Kevlar. Spiders make it at room temperature from water and protein, and we still cannot copy it properly.",
  [
    ["Liquid into thread", "Spider silk is a protein stored as liquid and turned into solid thread as it is drawn out, and one spider can make several kinds.",
      "Silk is made of proteins called spidroins, stored in the spider's abdomen as a thick liquid. As the spider draws it out through tiny spinnerets, changes in acidity and water content, plus the stretching force of pulling, make the molecules line up and lock into a solid fibre. It happens at normal temperature with water as the solvent, while human factories making strong fibres often need high heat and harsh chemicals.\n\nOrb-weavers, which build wheel-shaped webs, can have up to seven types of silk gland. Strong dragline silk forms the frame and safety line. The sticky capture spiral is stretchier and coated with glue droplets, so a fast insect is slowed rather than bouncing off. Other silks wrap prey, cement threads down and case eggs."],
    ["Stronger than steel?", "Dragline silk combines strength with stretch, making it exceptionally tough for its weight.",
      "The claim that spider silk is stronger than steel is often repeated, so let us be precise. Steel and good dragline silk have broadly similar strength per thickness, but silk is far lighter, so weight for weight silk comes out ahead. Its real superpower is toughness, the energy a material can absorb before breaking. Because dragline silk both resists pulling and stretches a good deal, it absorbs more energy than Kevlar, the fibre used in protective vests. The toughest silk known comes from Darwin's bark spider of Madagascar, described in 2010. It builds enormous webs over rivers, anchored by bridge lines that can stretch around 25 metres across the water."],
    ["Flying on silk", "Young spiders and some adults travel huge distances by floating on silk threads.",
      "Silk lets spiders fly. In a behaviour called ballooning, a spider climbs to a high point, raises its abdomen and releases fine threads into the air until it lifts off. Spiders have been found drifting thousands of metres up and landing on ships far out at sea, which helps explain how they are among the first animals to colonise new islands. Scientists long assumed ballooning depended only on wind. Research published in 2018 showed spiders also respond to the atmosphere's natural electric field, which can lift their charged silk. In parts of Australia, mass ballooning occasionally blankets paddocks in shimmering silk, sometimes nicknamed angel hair."],
    ["A million-spider cape", "Spider silk can be woven, but it is painstaking to harvest because spiders cannot be farmed like silkworms.",
      "People have long dreamed of spider silk cloth, but spiders are territorial and often cannibalistic, so they cannot be packed together like silkworms. A famous project in Madagascar showed what it takes. Textile maker Simon Peers and designer Nicholas Godley had workers collect more than a million golden orb-weaver spiders over several years, gently draw silk from each with a hand-cranked device, then return them to the wild. The naturally golden textiles, including a cape shown at London's Victoria and Albert Museum in 2012, are among the rarest fabrics in the world. Long before that, fine spider silk served as crosshairs in optical instruments such as telescope and gun sights, because it was thin, strong and even."],
    ["Copying the spider", "Engineers can make spider silk proteins in other organisms, but spinning them into fibre as good as the real thing remains hard.",
      "Since spiders cannot be farmed, scientists have tried to make silk without them. Researchers have inserted spider silk genes into bacteria, yeast, silkworms and plants, and in one well-known project, into goats whose milk contained silk proteins. Making the protein is the easier half. Spinning it into fibre as good as a spider's is very hard, because the spider's duct performs its chemistry and stretching with remarkable precision. Some companies have launched products using artificial silk proteins, but none yet matches natural dragline silk at large scale. Possible uses include medical sutures, artificial ligaments, lightweight protective gear and biodegradable materials. For now the humble garden spider still out-engineers the laboratory."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does liquid silk protein become a solid thread?", ["It is heated up inside the spider's abdomen","Acidity changes and pulling align proteins","It dries hard in sunlight after release","It reacts with oxygen in the air like glue"], 1, "Changes in acidity and water, combined with stretching as it is drawn out, make the proteins lock into a solid fibre."],
    [1, "What is the main strength of dragline silk compared with Kevlar?", ["Far greater resistance to very high heat","It is completely rigid and never stretches","Toughness: energy absorbed before breaking","Natural magnetic properties that repel metal"], 2, "Silk resists pulling and stretches, so it absorbs more energy than Kevlar before breaking."],
    [2, "What did the 2018 research add to our understanding of ballooning?", ["Spiders flap their front legs to take off","Only fully grown spiders are able to balloon","Ballooning only ever happens after dark","Electric fields in the air can help lift them"], 3, "Spiders sense electric fields that can provide lift, not just wind."],
    [3, "Why can't spiders be farmed like silkworms?", ["Their silk is poisonous to the touch","They are territorial and often cannibalistic","They die within hours of being captured","Farming spiders is illegal in most countries"], 1, "Crowded spiders attack and eat each other, so silk must be collected from individual spiders by hand."],
    [4, "Why has artificial spider silk not matched the natural fibre?", ["Scientists cannot make any spider silk protein","Spider genes cannot be put into other organisms","Spinning the protein into a fibre is very hard","Artificial silk always dissolves in water"], 2, "The spider's duct controls chemistry and stretching so precisely that it has proven very hard to copy."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Spider silk is made from water and protein at room temperature. Why can't we copy it?"});

WB.L("termite-mounds", "nature", "intermediate", "Termite mounds: cities without architects",
  "Millions of tiny, mostly blind insects build towers taller than a person, without plans or a boss. The way those towers breathe has even fooled scientists.",
  [
    ["Not white ants", "Termites are social cockroaches, not ants, and they digest wood with help from microbes in their guts.",
      "Termites are not ants. Genetic studies show they are specialised cockroaches that became social. Like ants, they have a strict division of labour: a king and queen produce the young, workers gather food and build, and soldiers defend, some with huge jaws and others with snouts that squirt sticky chemicals. Workers include both males and females, and young grow gradually without a pupa stage.\n\nTermites can live on wood and dead grass, even though cellulose is very hard to break down, because they have partners in their guts: bacteria and, in some groups, single-celled organisms. African and Asian Macrotermes go further, growing a fungus on chewed plant material inside their mounds and eating it. By recycling dead plants, termites enrich and loosen dry soils much as earthworms do in wetter ones."],
    ["Building without a blueprint", "Mounds emerge from simple rules followed by individual termites, a process called stigmergy.",
      "No termite knows what the finished mound will look like. Most workers are blind, and none is in charge. So how do they build such complex structures? In the 1950s French biologist Pierre-Paul Grassé proposed an idea he called stigmergy. Each insect responds to the work already done around it. A termite drops a mud pellet laced with scent, which makes other termites more likely to drop pellets in the same spot. Small piles grow into pillars, pillars bend towards each other and arches form. Local cues such as humidity, airflow and carbon dioxide also guide where they dig and build. The grand structure emerges from millions of tiny decisions, with no plan anywhere in the colony."],
    ["Top End compass mounds", "In the Northern Territory, so-called magnetic termites build thin mounds aligned north to south, which helps control temperature.",
      "Some of the world's most striking mounds are in northern Australia. In Litchfield National Park, near Darwin, fields of thin, slab-like mounds stand like gravestones, all aligned roughly north to south. They are built by so-called magnetic termites, a name that suggests a compass. Their broad faces catch the gentle morning and late-afternoon sun, while the thin edge faces the fierce midday sun, which helps keep the mound's temperature steadier through the day. Heat control is the best-supported reason for the alignment, though some experiments suggest the termites may also sense the Earth's magnetic field. Nearby, the giant cathedral mounds of other termite species can tower several metres high, among the largest structures built by any animal apart from humans."],
    ["How a mound breathes", "Mounds were long thought to work like chimneys, but research shows a more complex, lung-like system.",
      "A big colony uses lots of oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and heat, so its mound must exchange air with the outside. For decades the textbook explanation was a chimney: warm air from the colony rises up a central shaft and is drawn out, pulling fresh air in. Later work, notably by physiologist J. Scott Turner and colleagues, found this was too simple. Many mounds are closed structures with porous walls, more like a lung than a chimney. Research has shown that daily temperature swings, with the outer walls heating and cooling faster than the core, drive air around the channels, while wind gusts also push gases through the walls. Scientists are still refining the details."],
    ["Inspiring engineers", "A Zimbabwean building borrowed ideas from termite mounds to save energy.",
      "The best-known example of termite-inspired design is the Eastgate Centre in Harare, Zimbabwe, an office and shopping complex completed in 1996. Architect Mick Pearce, working with engineers, designed it to stay cool without conventional air conditioning. Thick walls store heat, fans move cool night air through the building and chimneys release warm air. It uses far less energy for cooling than comparable buildings. There is an irony: the design drew on the chimney model of termite ventilation, which biologists have since revised. Nature still holds plenty of lessons. In Brazil, scientists reported in 2018 a vast field of termite mounds covering an area around the size of Great Britain, some estimated to be about four thousand years old."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do termites digest tough plant material such as wood?", ["With stomach acid that dissolves cellulose","With help from gut microbes, and some farm fungus","By soaking it in water until it softens","By eating only the sap inside the wood"], 1, "Gut bacteria and other microbes break down cellulose, and Macrotermes also grow a fungus on chewed plants."],
    [1, "What is stigmergy?", ["A queen termite giving building orders","A poison sprayed by soldier termites","Each termite reacting to earlier building","The chemical that hardens termite mud"], 2, "Each termite reacts to cues like scented mud pellets, and the mound emerges from millions of local decisions."],
    [2, "Why are magnetic termite mounds aligned north to south?", ["To follow underground water","To block the prevailing winds","To mark the colony's borders","To avoid the hottest midday sun"], 3, "The alignment helps keep the mound's temperature steadier by limiting midday heating."],
    [3, "How does current research suggest many termite mounds ventilate?", ["Like a simple chimney with a constant updraft","Heat swings and wind push air through the walls","Worker termites fanning air with their wings","They stay sealed and need no fresh air"], 1, "Work by Turner and others showed mounds act more like lungs than chimneys."],
    [4, "How does the Eastgate Centre stay cool without conventional air conditioning?", ["Thick walls, night fans and warm-air chimneys","Water is sprayed over the roof all day long","Large ice stores underground hold winter cold","Its windows are sealed and tinted very dark"], 0, "The design borrowed from the termite mound idea of moving air through a structure, using ventilation chimneys."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Termite towers have no architect and no plan. How do they get built?"});

WB.L("island-evolution", "nature", "intermediate", "Island evolution: giants, dwarfs and the dodo",
  "Strand animals on an island and strange things happen. Elephants shrink, rats grow huge and birds forget how to fly.",
  [
    ["Natural laboratories", "Islands isolate living things, and Galapagos finches show how fast evolution can run there.",
      "Islands have taught biologists more about evolution than almost anywhere. Colonists that drift, fly or blow in are cut off from their relatives, often with few competitors and no large predators, and over generations they can change dramatically. Darwin's 1835 visit to the Galapagos helped shape his thinking, and Wallace developed similar ideas in the Malay Archipelago.\n\nGalapagos finches show adaptive radiation, where one ancestor spreads into many forms. Over about two million years, one colonising group became more than a dozen species, with beaks for crushing seeds, probing cactus flowers, catching insects or even drinking seabird blood. After a severe drought in 1977 left only large, hard seeds on Daphne Major, Peter and Rosemary Grant found that bigger-beaked birds survived better and the next generation had larger beaks. Evolution can be measured within years."],
    ["The island rule", "On islands, large animals often shrink while small animals often grow larger.",
      "Biologists have noticed a broad tendency nicknamed the island rule. Large mammals stranded on islands often become smaller over generations, perhaps because food is limited and there are fewer predators to outgrow. Small animals such as rodents often become larger, perhaps because they no longer need to hide from predators and face less competition. The fossil record is full of examples. Dwarf elephants, some about the size of a large pig, once lived on Mediterranean islands including Sicily, Malta and Crete. On the Indonesian island of Flores, there were dwarf relatives of elephants alongside giant rats. The rule is a tendency rather than a law, and scientists still debate how consistent it is, but the examples are striking."],
    ["The hobbit of Flores", "A small human species lived on Flores, and how it became so small is still debated.",
      "In 2003 researchers excavating Liang Bua cave on Flores found the skeleton of an adult human relative who stood about a metre tall. It was named Homo floresiensis and quickly nicknamed the hobbit. Its brain was about the size of a chimpanzee's, yet stone tools were found in the same cave layers. Later work dated the remains to between roughly 100,000 and 60,000 years ago. How the hobbits came to be so small is still debated. One idea is that they descended from Homo erectus and shrank under island conditions, like the island's elephants. Another is that they came from an older, already small-bodied lineage. Either way, island life shaped human evolution too."],
    ["Forgetting how to fly", "Without ground predators, island birds often lose flight, which leaves them defenceless when people arrive.",
      "Flying is expensive. It needs big muscles and a light, demanding body. On islands without ground predators, many birds evolved to give it up. The dodo of Mauritius was a large, flightless relative of pigeons, and its closest living relative is the Nicobar pigeon. New Zealand, with no native land mammals other than bats, became a kingdom of birds, including the giant moa, the kiwi and the kakapo, a heavy, flightless, nocturnal parrot. Losing flight was sensible until humans arrived with rats, cats, pigs and hunting. The dodo was gone within a century of Dutch sailors landing in 1598, with the last widely accepted sighting in 1662. The moa vanished within a few centuries of people reaching New Zealand."],
    ["Fragile and precious", "Island species are especially vulnerable to newcomers, but the Lord Howe stick insect shows they can be rescued.",
      "Island species often evolve without defences against newcomers, so they are especially vulnerable. A large share of recorded animal extinctions in the past five centuries have been island species, many caused by introduced rats, cats and other invaders. Australia has a famous case. On Lord Howe Island, a large, flightless stick insect disappeared after black rats came ashore from a shipwreck in 1918. In 2001, researchers found a tiny surviving population on Ball's Pyramid, a sheer volcanic spire in the ocean nearby. Captive breeding programs have since raised many more. In 2019 a major operation began to eradicate rodents from Lord Howe, opening the possibility of returning the insect home. Islands show both how easily species are lost and how they can be saved."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Peter and Rosemary Grant observe after the 1977 drought on Daphne Major?", ["All of the finches died out","The beaks became smaller overall","The next generation had larger beaks","Finches stopped breeding for years"], 2, "With only large, hard seeds left, bigger-beaked birds survived better and passed on larger beaks."],
    [1, "According to the island rule, what tends to happen to large mammals on islands?", ["They grow larger","They become smaller","They become flightless","They stay the same"], 1, "Large mammals often shrink on islands, as seen in the dwarf elephants of the Mediterranean and Flores."],
    [2, "Which statement about Homo floresiensis is accurate?", ["It was a modern human with a rare disease","Its small size is fully explained and agreed","It lived mainly on the island of Madagascar","Its origins and small size are still debated"], 3, "Whether through island dwarfing or an older small lineage, its origins are still debated."],
    [3, "Why did many island birds lose the ability to fly?", ["Flying is costly, and nothing on land hunted them","Island winds were too strong for them to fly in","Their wings were damaged by constant salt water","Early humans bred them to be flightless"], 0, "Flying needs big muscles and a light body, so birds gave it up where nothing hunted them on the ground."],
    [4, "Why are island species so vulnerable to introduced animals such as rats?", ["They breed too slowly to matter much","They are always much smaller than invaders","They evolved with no defences against them","Introduced animals mostly eat only plants"], 2, "Island species often have no defences against predators they have never met, which is why so many have gone extinct."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"On islands, elephants shrink and rats grow huge. What does that say about evolution?"});

WB.L("symbiosis", "nature", "advanced", "Symbiosis: the partnerships that built life",
  "Nearly every cell in your body contains descendants of ancient bacteria. Life on Earth is less a war of all against all than a tangle of deals, partnerships and double-crosses.",
  [
    ["Deals, not just wars", "Symbiosis is a spectrum of close partnerships, from mutual benefit to parasitism, renegotiated as conditions change.",
      "Biologists sort close living-together relationships by who benefits. In mutualism, both partners gain: bees collect nectar while pollinating flowers. In commensalism, one gains while the other is barely affected, like barnacles riding on a whale. In parasitism, one gains at the other's expense. The German botanist Anton de Bary coined the word symbiosis in 1879.\n\nThese boxes are useful, but nature rarely respects them. A relationship can turn from helpful to harmful as conditions change, and each partner is under pressure to take a little more than it gives. Symbiosis is best seen as a spectrum of bargains, constantly renegotiated by evolution."],
    ["Bacteria inside your cells", "Mitochondria and chloroplasts descend from free-living bacteria that were engulfed over a billion years ago.",
      "Life's most important partnership began well over a billion years ago. A single-celled organism took in a bacterium and, instead of digesting it, came to depend on it. That bacterium became the mitochondrion, which powers our cells using oxygen. Later, an early cell took in a photosynthetic bacterium, which became the chloroplast in plants and algae.\n\nIn the 1960s, the American biologist Lynn Margulis championed this idea, called endosymbiosis, against widespread scepticism. The evidence now backs her. Mitochondria and chloroplasts have their own small circular DNA, bacteria-like ribosomes and double membranes, and they divide by splitting in two. Complex life, including us, is a partnership at its core."],
    ["Lichens and corals", "Lichens and corals are partnerships between very different organisms, and corals lose theirs when the water gets too hot.",
      "A lichen looks like a single organism, but it is a fungus living with an alga or a cyanobacterium. The fungus provides structure and shelter, and its partner makes food from sunlight. Together they colonise bare rock, tree bark and frozen tundra where neither could live alone. In 2016, researchers found that many lichens also contain a yeast, a third partner that went unnoticed for over a century.\n\nReef-building corals work similarly. Their cells house microscopic algae that photosynthesise and pass much of their food to the coral. When the water becomes too hot, the partnership breaks down. The coral expels the algae and turns white, an event known as coral bleaching."],
    ["Deals and double-crosses", "Tight partnerships such as figs and fig wasps invite cheats, as cleaner fish and their mimics show.",
      "A fig starts as an enclosed cluster of tiny flowers, and only a fig wasp, usually specialised to one or a few fig species, can pollinate it. A female squeezes in, often losing her wings, pollinates some flowers, lays eggs in others and dies. Her offspring mate inside, and the females leave carrying pollen. Neither partner can reproduce without the other, and the bond is tens of millions of years old.\n\nPartnerships invite cheating. Bluestreak cleaner wrasse remove parasites from larger fish, but would often rather nibble their protective mucus. Clients chase cheats away, and cleaners behave better when other fish are watching. The sabre-toothed blenny goes further: it mimics a cleaner, then bites chunks from fish."],
    ["Partners close to home", "Our own species has partners too: honeyguide birds lead people to honey, and gut microbes and Wolbachia bacteria shape our health.",
      "In parts of Africa, the greater honeyguide leads people to wild bees' nests, then eats the wax left once humans open the hive. Yao honey hunters in northern Mozambique summon the bird with a special trilling call, and a 2016 study found it responded to that call far more than to other sounds.\n\nOur microbes matter too. Trillions of bacteria, mostly in the gut, help digest food, train the immune system and fend off germs. The claim that they outnumber our cells ten to one is a myth: a 2016 study put the ratio closer to one to one. Wolbachia bacteria introduced into Aedes aegypti mosquitoes reduce their ability to spread dengue. The World Mosquito Program, which began in Australia, released them in north Queensland, where local outbreaks have since become very rare."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is commensalism?", ["One partner gains at the other's expense","One partner benefits, the other barely affected","Both partners gain from the relationship","Neither partner is affected by the other"], 1, "In commensalism one partner benefits while the other is barely affected, unlike mutualism, where both gain."],
    [1, "What evidence supports the idea that mitochondria were once free-living bacteria?", ["They are found only in plant cells","They are made of the same material as cell walls","They have their own circular DNA, like bacteria","They can still survive outside cells today"], 2, "Mitochondria keep bacteria-like features: their own small circular DNA, ribosomes, double membranes and division by splitting."],
    [2, "What happens to a coral during bleaching?", ["It expels the algae living in its cells","It grows a thick white shell","It starts to photosynthesise by itself","It is buried under drifting sand"], 0, "When the water is too hot, the coral-algae partnership breaks down and the coral expels its algae, turning white."],
    [3, "What does the sabre-toothed blenny do?", ["It cleans parasites off larger fish","It guards coral from hungry predators","It pollinates sea grass in shallow bays","It mimics a cleaner, then bites clients"], 3, "Every successful partnership creates an opening for cheats, and the blenny exploits the cleaner's good reputation."],
    [4, "What is true of the claim that bacteria outnumber our own cells ten to one?", ["It is accurate and well established","It is a myth; the ratio is closer to one to one","It is a myth; our own cells outnumber bacteria a hundred to one","It is accurate for the gut only"], 1, "A 2016 study estimated roughly equal numbers of bacterial and human cells, debunking the old ten-to-one figure."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Nearly every cell in your body hosts descendants of ancient bacteria. How?"});

